1.1.0 (2026-11-20)
- The holotape now opens on a menu like the Pip-Boy's own lists: START, PAYOUTS and CONTROLS on the left, Luck's Vault Boy with a line about the selected row on the right, and your bet and biggest win under them. Either wheel moves through it (it is one up-and-down list); a press opens.
- CONTROLS opens a one-page controls sheet (the same as the README table); any press goes back to the menu.
- The controls line is gone from under the machine, and PRESS: BACK from under the payout table. Holding the left wheel at the machine now goes back to the menu, where PAYOUTS is (it used to open the payout table directly); betting and spinning are unchanged. The payout table goes back to the menu.
- Opening the holotape with no caps left shows the menu first; START then shows the OUT OF CAPS screen as before.
- After a losing spin that leaves fewer caps than your bet, the screen now shows the bet the next spin will really take. Before, it kept showing the old bet (for example BET 25 with 5 caps left) and the next spin quietly took 5. Found in a full scripted playthrough (241 spins over 7 games, every payout checked against the table).
- The startup loader that brings this holotape's hook back after a restart (loader v4) runs each hook with new Function instead of eval. With eval, every hook that stays switched on (the STATS rows, the MISC hand-off, the journal in DATA) kept the loader's working space, and with it the hook's whole source text, in memory for as long as the Pip-Boy was on. Measured in a desktop copy of the Pip-Boy's own JavaScript engine (Espruino 2v29): all our hooks together held about 970 blocks after a restart; now about 170. Nothing in the Pip-Boy's own firmware or menus changes. The first restart after updating still uses the old loader once and replaces it.
- Opening the holotape no longer runs its STATS hook again when the hook is already running and listed for startup. The check looked for an old global (RM_STATS) that the hooks stopped setting, so the hook ran on every open; it now checks RM_PATCH like the other hooks.
- The STATS > GENERAL record rows can no longer be changed with the right wheel. That screen is the Pip-Boy's own editable list and it saves the whole list to SETTINGS/GENERAL.JSON when you leave it, so a turned wheel changed the record shown and saved a copy of our rows into the Pip-Boy's settings. Our rows are now handed to the screen read-only and are left out of that save; the Pip-Boy's own rows still change and save as before, and copies of our rows saved earlier are dropped the next time the screen saves. Checked against the Pip-Boy's own GENERAL screen code in the desktop Espruino simulator.
- The startup loader that keeps the STATS hook working after a restart is quieter and more careful (after Aidan's Lab's review of the firmware-update problem): it never prints or logs anything to the USB console, so it can no longer upset a firmware update or the companion app; it waits for the Pip-Boy's own code (Pip, the screen, the list maker and the settings reader) to finish starting before running any hook, and if that never happens it quietly gives up after a minute instead; it runs only once per start; and errors are kept in RM_HOLO_ERR for the terminal instead.
- Opening the tape upgrades the old loader in place. The loader carries a version, so newer and older hooks no longer take turns rewriting it, and hooks from before v2 are skipped at startup until their tape is updated.
- The hook frees its setup code once it has run, so it holds less memory while the Pip-Boy is on.
- The STATS hook now keeps almost nothing in memory. Instead of every game keeping its own row code loaded all the time (about 30 blocks each on the Pip-Boy), the rows are read from the games' save files only while STATS > GENERAL opens. Hooks from older versions are not run there.
- The left wheel now works like the rest of the tapes: up makes the bet bigger, down makes it smaller. It no longer clicks when the bet can't change.
- The payout table is laid out like the Pip-Boy's own lists, with Vault Boy (Luck) in a panel on the right.
- The controls hint sits a little higher, clear of the rounded bottom corners.
- The STATS/MISC/DATA hook keeps working when the Pip-Boy's internal storage is full: a failed boot-slot write ("Unable to find or create file") no longer stops it switching on; opening the tape again after a full restart switches it back on.
- OUT OF CAPS is now its own screen with Luck's Vault Boy and your biggest win, in the same layout as the other games' results, instead of a box drawn over the reels.
- The bet shown when you open the machine is one you can cover (a saved bet of 10 with 1 cap left showed BET 10).
- The controls hint is shorter and placed higher, clear of the rounded bottom corners; the payout table moved up to make room for its return hint.
- The bottle cap beside your caps no longer cuts into the top of the cabinet.
- Fixed a possible OUT OF MEMORY crash when starting from ITEMS > MISC: the Pip-Boy art and the STATS hook now load just after the game opens, once the Pip-Boy has freed the MISC list.
- The STATS hook creates the USER folder when the SD card has none, so it can register for startup instead of failing with NO_PATH (the record row now shows on cards without a USER folder).
- Your record shows in the Pip-Boy's own STATS > GENERAL screen. The hook ships with the holotape as HOLO/ATOMIC_SLOTS/STATS_HOOK.JS; it switches on when you open Atomic Slots and registers itself in one shared boot slot (.boot1-.boot3, never .boot0) so it keeps working after a full restart. No CFW file or Service Records holotape needed.

1.0.1 (2026-11-08)
- A full or write-locked SD card no longer crashes the game: a save that cannot be written is skipped.

1.0.0 (2026-10-17)
https://github.com/CodyTolene/pip-boy-3000-holotapes/pull/122
- Initial release.
- Header drawn in the Pip-Boy's own layout (title on the line, info underneath), so nothing is cut off by the rounded corners of the screen.
- Icon is now a Pip-Boy .img, so it shows in ITEMS > MISC.
- The CAP symbol and the caps counter use a real bottle cap, and the payout table shows Vault Boy (Luck), drawn from the Pip-Boy's own game data in Fallout 3 or New Vegas mode. Falls back to the plain look if the data is missing.
- Initial release of Atomic Slots, a three-reel slot machine with five bet sizes, a payout table, and a saved wallet.
